    "response_type": "answer",
    "answer_text": "In the Calvin cycle, RuBisCO catalyzes a reaction between CO2 and RuBP, the five-carbon molecule that starts the cycle, forming a six-carbon compound that is immediately converted into two three-carbon compounds; this is carbon fixation, because CO2 is fixed from its inorganic form into organic molecules [ev_007]. The three-carbon compound 3-PGA is then converted into G3P by ATP and NADPH, which use their stored energy; this is a reduction reaction because it involves the gain of electrons [ev_007]. Because one carbon-fixation turn yields G3P molecules that are used in regeneration, only one G3P molecule leaves the cycle per three turns, to contribute to the formation of carbohydrate or other compounds needed by the plant, while the remaining G3P molecules stay in the cycle to be used to regenerate RuBP so the system can fix more CO2 [ev_002]. The carbohydrate molecule has six carbon atoms, so it takes six turns of the Calvin cycle to make one carbohydrate molecule, with one turn for each carbon dioxide molecule fixed [ev_001]. The G3P that remains in the cycle is used to regenerate RuBP, which is ready to react with more CO2 [ev_003].",
